  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "/gry15Ld6ZprQVXTg8Zh/AdSypc=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:18:43.263Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Every operation is keyed by branch as well as document and scope, and `main` is the default \u2014 typically the published version. Custom branches are described as drafts, experiments or parallel versions, and operations are **isolated per branch**.\n\nWhat does not exist yet is the machinery to use them: the architecture states plainly that **branching and merging operations are planned but not yet implemented.** So the storage layer will hold operations on any branch name a caller supplies, and nothing will create a branch from another, diff two branches, or merge one into another.\n\nFor anyone designing on top of this: a draft/published workflow can be *stored* today by writing to a second branch name, but the promotion step has to be implemented in application code (re-submitting the actions against `main`), and there is no reactor-level guarantee about how the two histories relate.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/reactor/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` \u2014 Core Concepts \u203a Branch\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:18:43.262Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:18:43.263Z"
    }
  },
  {
